JSON-LD, also known as JSON Linked Data, is a way to provide structured data to search engines. It is a standard format for describing the content of a web page for better indexing and results.This plugin emits JSON-LD as &lt;script type=&quot;application/ld+json&quot;&gt; blocks. Enable the types you need in site.jsonLd and add per-page data in pages[].jsonLd.

The format is pretty well standardized at json-ld.org and the vocabulary for what you can describe lives at schema.org. The plugin models the common types from schema.org; anything not covered goes through extra! If you feel that any type is missing or should be added to the plugin, please open an issue or PR, I’d love to add it!
Organization
site: {
  jsonLd: { organization: true },
}

This emits an Organization block with the site name and URL. Add a logo:
site: {
  name: &quot;My Site&quot;,
  jsonLd: { organization: { logo: &quot;https://example.com/logo.png&quot; } },
}

WebSite
site: {
  jsonLd: { website: true },
}

Emits a WebSite block. Set site.searchAction to add a search box to the result:
site: {
  jsonLd: {
    website: {
      searchAction: {
        target: &quot;https://example.com/search?q={query}&quot;,
        queryInput: &quot;required name=query&quot;,
      },
    },
  },
}

Article
pages: {
  &quot;/blog/hello-world&quot;: {
    jsonLd: {
      article: {
        headline: &quot;Hello, world&quot;,
        type: &quot;BlogPosting&quot;,
        author: { name: &quot;Alice&quot; },
        datePublished: &quot;2024-01-15&quot;,
        dateModified: &quot;2024-06-20&quot;,
        description: &quot;My first post on this site.&quot;,
        image: &quot;https://example.com/post.png&quot;,
      },
    },
  },
}

Note: author can be a single object or an array. type defaults to Article; set BlogPosting, NewsArticle or others as needed.
Product
pages: {
  &quot;/products/widget&quot;: {
    jsonLd: {
      product: {
        name: &quot;Widget&quot;,
        image: &quot;https://example.com/widget.png&quot;,
        description: &quot;A widget for doing things.&quot;,
        sku: &quot;WIDGET-001&quot;,
        brand: &quot;My Brand&quot;,
        mpn: &quot;MPN-001&quot;,
        offers: {
          price: 19.99,
          priceCurrency: &quot;USD&quot;,
          availability: &quot;InStock&quot;,
          url: &quot;https://example.com/products/widget&quot;,
        },
        review: {
          reviewRating: { ratingValue: 5, bestRating: 5 },
          author: { name: &quot;Bob&quot; },
          reviewBody: &quot;Best widget I have owned.&quot;,
        },
      },
    },
  },
}

BreadcrumbList
pages: {
  &quot;/products/widget&quot;: {
    jsonLd: {
      breadcrumb: [
        { name: &quot;Home&quot;, item: &quot;https://example.com/&quot; },
        { name: &quot;Products&quot;, item: &quot;https://example.com/products&quot; },
        { name: &quot;Widget&quot;, item: &quot;https://example.com/products/widget&quot; },
      ],
    },
  },
}

ImageObject
pages: {
  &quot;/gallery/photo-1&quot;: {
    jsonLd: {
      image: {
        url: &quot;https://example.com/photo-1.jpg&quot;,
        description: &quot;A photo of a thing&quot;,
        license: &quot;https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/&quot;,
        author: { name: &quot;Alice&quot; },
      },
    },
  },
}

Extra
If the plugin does not cover a type you need, pass raw JSON-LD through extra:
jsonLd: {
  extra: [
    {
      &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;HowTo&quot;,
      name: &quot;How to do the thing&quot;,
      step: [{ &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;HowToStep&quot;, text: &quot;Do the thing&quot; }],
    },
  ],
}

extra is emitted verbatim. It is what you would call an “escape hatch” for types the plugin does not model. If you feel that any type is missing or should be added to the plugin, please open an issue or PR.

39.text:
Open Graph, also known as og:* tags, is a universal standard for describing how a page looks when shared on social media. It’s used by Facebook, LinkedIn, Discord, Slack (oh hi Hack Club), and many other platforms. Suffice to say, they’re important.
The spec exists at ogp.me.

Side note: In my opinion, that’s how spec pages should be in my opinion, as it’s pretty easy to parse, not 20 pages and written in legalese lol.

They are emitted from the openGraph block, which inherits from site defaults.
Basic tags
pages: {
  &quot;/&quot;: {
    openGraph: {
      title: &quot;My Site&quot;,
      description: &quot;My awesome site&quot;,
      type: &quot;website&quot;,
      url: &quot;https://example.com&quot;,
      image: &quot;https://example.com/og.png&quot;,
      siteName: &quot;My Site&quot;,
      locale: &quot;en_US&quot;,
    },
  },
}


type defaults to website. Set article for blog posts, book, profile, music.*, video.* or others… based on what your page is about.
url defaults to the page canonical
image defaults to site.image

Article type
openGraph: {
  type: &quot;article&quot;,
  article: {
    publishedTime: &quot;2024-01-15&quot;,
    modifiedTime: &quot;2024-06-20&quot;,
    author: &quot;https://example.com/authors/alice&quot;,
    section: &quot;Technology&quot;,
    tag: [&quot;typescript&quot;, &quot;vite&quot;],
  },
}

author can be a single URL or an array. tag is the same.
Book type

openGraph: {
  type: &quot;book&quot;,
  book: {
    author: &quot;https://example.com/authors/popper&quot;,
    isbn: &quot;978-3-16-148410-0&quot;,
    releaseDate: &quot;2019-02-01&quot;,
    tag: [&quot;fiction&quot;],
  },
}

Profile type
openGraph: {
  type: &quot;profile&quot;,
  profile: {
    firstName: &quot;Karl&quot;,
    lastName: &quot;Popper&quot;,
    username: &quot;karlpopper&quot;,
    gender: &quot;male&quot;,
  },
}

Music types
music.album, music.playlist and music.radio_station share the same fields:
openGraph: {
  type: &quot;music.album&quot;,
  music: {
    duration: 213,
    musician: [&quot;https://example.com/artists/queen&quot;],
    song: [{ url: &quot;https://example.com/songs/we-will-rock-you&quot;, disc: 1, track: 1 }],
    releaseDate: &quot;1977-10-21&quot;,
  },
}

song and album are arrays of { url, disc?, track? }. disc and track default to 1.
Video types
video.movie, video.episode and video.series:
openGraph: {
  type: &quot;video.movie&quot;,
  video: {
    actor: [{ url: &quot;https://example.com/people/tom-hanks&quot;, role: &quot;Forrest Gump&quot; }],
    director: [&quot;https://example.com/people/robert-zemeckis&quot;],
    duration: 142 * 60 + 24,
    releaseDate: &quot;1994-06-23&quot;,
    series: &quot;https://example.com/series/futurama&quot;,
  },
}

Structured images, audio and video
Instead of a single image URL, pass structured entries with extra properties:
openGraph: {
  images: [
    { url: &quot;https://example.com/og-1200x630.png&quot;, width: 1200, height: 630, alt: &quot;Share this page&quot; },
  ],
  audios: [{ url: &quot;https://example.com/podcast.mp3&quot; }],
  videos: [{ url: &quot;https://example.com/trailer.mp4&quot; }],
}

Structured images override the flat image field when present.
payment.link
openGraph: {
  type: &quot;payment.link&quot;,
  payment: {
    description: &quot;One month subscription&quot;,
    currency: &quot;USD&quot;,
    amount: 9.99,
    status: &quot;PENDING&quot;,
    id: &quot;sub_123&quot;,
    successUrl: &quot;https://example.com/thanks&quot;,
  },
}

determiner
openGraph: {
  determiner: &quot;auto&quot;;
}

Valid values: &quot;auto&quot;, &quot;none&quot;, &quot;noun&quot;. A determiner is the word that comes before the title (noun), like “a,” “an,” or “the.” By default, it’s set to auto, which means the system decides whether or not to include it.

56.text:
A sitemap is an XML file that lists all the pages on your site. It’s extremely useful for search engines, as it’s a easily parsible list of all your pages. It can also include images, videos and news articles.
The protocol is documented at sitemaps.org. I tried to follow this as closely as possible, but if you notice any discrepancies, please open an issue or PR!!!

sitemap.xml is generated at build time from the pages map. It lists every route that is not noindex and not in exclude.
Basic options
sitemap: {
  exclude: [&quot;/admin&quot;, &quot;/login&quot;],
  maxUrls: 100_000,
  defaultChangefreq: &quot;weekly&quot;,
  defaultPriority: 0.8,
  siteUrl: &quot;https://example.com&quot;,
}


exclude: routes to leave out
maxUrls: URLs per sitemap file. Default 50000
defaultChangefreq: fallback for pages without one
defaultPriority: fallback for pages without one
siteUrl: origin used to enforce a single-host sitemap

changefreq
Per-page or global and valid values: always, hourly, daily, weekly, monthly, yearly, never.
pages: {
  &quot;/blog/hello-world&quot;: { changefreq: &quot;weekly&quot;, priority: 0.9 },
  &quot;/&quot;: { changefreq: &quot;daily&quot;, priority: 1.0 },
}

maxUrls and splitting
Past maxUrls URLs, the plugin splits into multiple files: sitemap.xml, sitemap-1.xml, sitemap-2.xml, and so on. The index file lists them all and is the only one that you provide to Google Search Console or other search engine tools.
The default of 50000 is the sitemaps.org recommendation. Most sites should never hit it. Raise it if you have a large site and want fewer files!
Image namespace
When a page has openGraph.image (or structured images), the sitemap entry gets &lt;image:image&gt;:
&lt;url&gt;
  &lt;loc&gt;https://example.com/blog/hello-world&lt;/loc&gt;
  &lt;lastmod&gt;2024-06-20&lt;/lastmod&gt;
  &lt;changefreq&gt;weekly&lt;/changefreq&gt;
  &lt;priority&gt;0.9&lt;/priority&gt;
  &lt;image:image&gt;
    &lt;image:loc&gt;https://example.com/post.png&lt;/image:loc&gt;
    &lt;image:title&gt;Hello, world&lt;/image:title&gt;
    &lt;image:caption&gt;My first post on this site.&lt;/image:caption&gt;
  &lt;/image:image&gt;
&lt;/url&gt;

image:title comes from the page title. image:caption from the page description
Video namespace
When a page has openGraph.video, the sitemap entry gets &lt;video:video&gt;:
&lt;url&gt;
  &lt;loc&gt;https://example.com/watch/trailer&lt;/loc&gt;
  &lt;video:video&gt;
    &lt;video:thumbnail_loc&gt;https://example.com/thumb.png&lt;/video:thumbnail_loc&gt;
    &lt;video:title&gt;Trailer&lt;/video:title&gt;
    &lt;video:description&gt;Watch the trailer.&lt;/video:description&gt;
    &lt;video:content_loc&gt;https://example.com/trailer.mp4&lt;/video:content_loc&gt;
    &lt;video:duration&gt;142&lt;/video:duration&gt;
  &lt;/video:video&gt;
&lt;/url&gt;

A thumbnail is required for video entries! Pages without one are automatically skipped rather than emitting invalid XML.
News namespace
When a page sets openGraph.article.publishedTime, the sitemap entry gets a &lt;news:news&gt; block:
&lt;url&gt;
  &lt;loc&gt;https://example.com/blog/breaking&lt;/loc&gt;
  &lt;news:news&gt;
    &lt;news:publication&gt;
      &lt;news:name&gt;My Site&lt;/news:name&gt;
      &lt;news:language&gt;en&lt;/news:language&gt;
    &lt;/news:publication&gt;
    &lt;news:publication_date&gt;2024-06-20&lt;/news:publication_date&gt;
    &lt;news:title&gt;Breaking&lt;/news:title&gt;
  &lt;/news:news&gt;
&lt;/url&gt;

xhtml:link alternates
Pages with hreflang alternates get &lt;xhtml:link&gt; entries in their sitemap URL:
&lt;url&gt;
  &lt;loc&gt;https://example.com/&lt;/loc&gt;
  &lt;xhtml:link rel=&quot;alternate&quot; hreflang=&quot;en&quot; href=&quot;https://example.com/&quot;/&gt;
  &lt;xhtml:link rel=&quot;alternate&quot; hreflang=&quot;es&quot; href=&quot;https://example.com/es/&quot;/&gt;
  &lt;xhtml:link rel=&quot;alternate&quot; hreflang=&quot;x-default&quot; href=&quot;https://example.com/&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/url&gt;

Single-host enforcement
All sitemap URLs must share the same host as site.url. If a page canonical points elsewhere, the entry is skipped. This keeps the sitemap valid. Open an issue/PR if you want to allow cross-host sitemaps.

65.text:
SPA apps have a problem. If you navigate to a new route, the &lt;title&gt; and &lt;meta&gt; tags do not update. This is because the plugin only injects them at build time.So, the plugin injects tags at build time for the initial HTML. When the client navigates to a new route, the tags do not update on their own so you need to call applySeo().
import { applySeo } from &quot;vite-plugin-seo-meta/runtime&quot;;

function onRouteChange() {
  applySeo({ title: &quot;New Page&quot;, description: &quot;...&quot; });
}

How it works
applySeo(page, site?) merges the page data with the injected site config. Pass site explicitly when you need a different site than the one the plugin injected. Omit it and the plugin uses the config from the build.
It only updates head elements the plugin manages and your own tags are left alone once the page is loaded!
What it updates

&lt;title&gt;
&lt;meta name=&quot;description&quot;&gt;
&lt;meta name=&quot;robots&quot;&gt;
&lt;link rel=&quot;canonical&quot;&gt;
Open Graph tags
Twitter Card tags
JSON-LD scripts

When to call it
Call it after every route change. In a router, that is the navigation callback:
// vue-router
router.afterEach((to) =&gt; {
  applySeo({ title: to.meta.title, description: to.meta.description });
});

// react-router
useEffect(() =&gt; {
  applySeo({ title: location.pathname, description: &quot;...&quot; });
}, [location]);

What it does not do
applySeo updates tags. It does not push history, change the URL, or update the browser title bar. Your router handles that! Don’t just rely on applySeo if you’re making an SPA (if that wasn’t obvious already)!
It also does not fetch data, as you should already have it available. Pass the metadata you already have!

